WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.486
An XSS-resistant ASP.

2
00:00:01.486 --> 00:00:07.855
NET Core workflow starts by tracking untrusted or unreviewed data from entry to rendering.

3
00:00:07.855 --> 00:00:18.682
Forms, APIs, files, databases, CMS fields, partner feeds, support messages, and operational logs may all provide content that later appears in a browser.

4
00:00:18.682 --> 00:00:25.547
Knowing where that content moves helps teams make the right rendering decision at the right time.

5
00:00:25.547 --> 00:00:26.962
Use Razor and ASP.

6
00:00:26.962 --> 00:00:28.943
NET Core defaults correctly.

7
00:00:28.943 --> 00:00:33.826
Standard encoded output is a strong baseline for normal text display.

8
00:00:33.826 --> 00:00:40.054
Avoid unsafe escape hatches unless there is a specific reviewed need for trusted markup.

9
00:00:40.054 --> 00:00:49.253
When markup is allowed, document the source of trust, the sanitizer or policy involved, and every display path that depends on it.

10
00:00:49.253 --> 00:00:51.730
Encode for the destination context.

11
00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:58.948
HTML text, attributes, URLs, scripts, styles, JSON, and client-side rendering are not interchangeable.

12
00:00:58.948 --> 00:01:05.883
Prefer framework-supported helpers, serializers, and binding patterns over custom string handling.

13
00:01:05.883 --> 00:01:11.332
When a safer design avoids a risky context entirely, choose the safer design.

14
00:01:11.332 --> 00:01:19.400
Treat rich text, JavaScript clients, APIs, UI dependencies, shared components, and legacy views as review targets.

15
00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:24.566
These areas often sit at the boundary between data and browser structure.

16
00:01:24.566 --> 00:01:35.464
Clear contracts between backend and frontend teams reduce assumptions about whether a field is plain text, a URL, an identifier, or approved rich content.

17
00:01:35.464 --> 00:01:46.998
Finally, keep protections active through documentation, testing, dependency review, CSP and browser-side controls, operational monitoring, and ongoing maintenance.

18
00:01:46.998 --> 00:01:49.900
XSS prevention is not a one-time cleanup.

19
00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:57.967
It is a set of ordinary engineering habits that make safe rendering easier to preserve as the application changes.
